# Project language

- Write user-facing labels, definitions, explanations, examples, and help text in Roman Urdu using the Latin alphabet.
- Keep product names, editor names, technical terms, and acronyms in English when that is clearer.
- Use English prose only when the user explicitly asks for it.
- Make pages as graphical and visual as practical. Use cards, icons, diagrams, dividers, and connector lines to organize content and show relationships.
- Prefer line-based visual structure when it helps explain connections, comparisons, or hierarchy.
- Whenever an abbreviation or acronym appears in any lesson, also add a clear, contextual entry for it in the Abbreviations tab. Audit existing lessons for missing entries; use the correct expansion when one exists, and label names or disputed expansions accurately instead of inventing a full form.
- Every explanation or definition must be paired with at least one concrete example in the same lesson/card. Add examples in Roman Urdu, keep them specific to the concept, and avoid repeating a generic example across unrelated terms. Every glossary item must have its own `example` value or a matching entry in the shared glossary-example map.
- When the user supplies an incorrect or personal/mnemonic definition, preserve their original wording as a clearly labeled “Aapka original note” and place the accurate standard meaning beside it, with a concrete example. Never silently replace the user's wording.
- Keep a separate `LINKS.md` register of user-shared resource URLs and their stated use/examples. Add relevant links to the Physics tab's “Links & Resources” chapter as well; deduplicate repeated URLs, record uncertain purposes as uncertain, and do not imply endorsement. Never copy credentials, API keys, auth tokens, or private phone numbers from a link into the public-facing app or register; redact them and alert the user if a credential was exposed.
